	<text xml:lang="en">
		<body>
<div xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0"><p>Noninvasive genetic sampling (NGS) has become indispensable tools for wildlife biologists. Technical improvements aim to reduce the genotyping error rates and thus generate more reliable conclusions <ref type="bibr" target="#b0">(Bonin et al. 2004;</ref><ref type="bibr" target="#b12">Pompanon et al. 2005;</ref><ref type="bibr" target="#b16">Waits and Paetkau 2005)</ref>. However, there is mounting concern that genotyping errors may bias the results and negatively influence the management of threatened and endangered species <ref type="bibr" target="#b15">(Taberlet et al. 1999;</ref><ref type="bibr" target="#b2">Fernando et al. 2003;</ref><ref type="bibr" target="#b5">Hoffman and Amos 2005)</ref>. Efficient fecal DNA preservation has proved to be essential in recovering high quantities of high quality DNA, which increase the success rate of PCR and reduce genotyping error rates <ref type="bibr" target="#b18">(Wehausen et al. 2004;</ref><ref type="bibr" target="#b10">Piggott 2004;</ref><ref type="bibr" target="#b8">Nsubuga et al. 2004;</ref><ref type="bibr" target="#b4">Ha ´jkova ´et al. 2006</ref>). However, no universally effective and applicable preservation method has been developed, and methods can be inconsistently successful across different species and studies <ref type="bibr" target="#b17">(Wasser et al. 1997;</ref><ref type="bibr" target="#b6">Murphy et al. 2000</ref><ref type="bibr" target="#b7">Murphy et al. , 2002;;</ref><ref type="bibr" target="#b3">Frantzen et al. 1998;</ref><ref type="bibr" target="#b11">Piggott and Taylor 2003;</ref><ref type="bibr" target="#b9">Panasci et al. 2011)</ref>.</p><p>Starting in 1985, Przewalski's horses were reintroduced to China. However, genetic monitoring is still insufficient for the captive and released populations because of the difficulty of obtaining adequate DNA samples. Here we evaluate three fecal DNA preservation methods and two extraction methods for their suitability for the analysis of reintroduced Przewalski's horses.</p><p>Twenty fresh fecal samples were collected. Each fecal sample was divided into three parts, and each part was preserved using one of the following three methods. (1) Drying, (2) Ethanol, (3) Freezing. Fecal DNA extraction was carried out using TIANamp Stool DNA Kit (TIANGEN, Beijing) and the manual method (see supplementary file 1). Four horse-specific microsatellite loci (HTG08, CA425, HMS07, VHL20; <ref type="bibr" target="#b13">Shiue et al. 1999)</ref> were examined. Each 25 lL PCR mixture contained 1.5 mM MgCl 2 , 2.5 lL 109 buffer, 0.2 mM dNTPs, 0.4 lM of each primer pair, 0.2 lL 10 mg/mL BSA, 0.4 lL Taq polymerase (5U/lL, TaKaRa) and 2 lL fecal DNA. PCR conditions were as follows: 5 min at 95 °C followed by 35 cycles of 45 s at 95 °C, 1 min at the annealing temperature and 2 min at 72 °C, then 72 °C for 10 min. To optimize the combination of preservation and extraction methods, a two-way analysis of variance (ANOVA) was applied to ranked data <ref type="bibr" target="#b1">(Dytham 2011)</ref>.</p><p>We evaluated the effects of preservation time (1 week, 2, 3, 6 and 9 months) on PCR success rates using Cochran test and McNemar's test. Preservation effectiveness was evaluated after 1 week, 2, 3 and 6 months of preservation time by calculating allelic dropout (ADO) and false alleles (FA) using Wilcoxon's signed rank test (see experiment design in Table <ref type="table" target="#tab_0">1</ref>). Fecal genotypes were established by 8 % polyacrylamide gels based on the multiple-tubes approach <ref type="bibr" target="#b14">(Taberlet et al. 1996</ref>). An allele was accepted only if it had been recorded at least twice (e.g. loci that gave rise to the same heterozygous genotype twice were accepted). In the ambiguous cases, a further four PCRs were performed to yield genotypes. Here, homozygote consensus genotypes were scored only if the same allele was documented all seven times. Samples for which genotypes could not be assigned after seven positive PCRs were classified as 'unknown'.</p><p>For the six trials of combined preservation and extraction methods, the preservation method had a significant effect on the PCR success rate (F (2,114) = 7.234, P = 0.027). Feces preserved with drying and freezing yielded lower PCR success rates than samples preserved with ethanol (Fig. <ref type="figure" target="#fig_0">1</ref>). The interaction term between preservation and extraction was significant (F (2,114) = 22.111, P = 0.009). In comparison, the highest amplification success rate (0.661) was obtained with samples stored in ethanol and extracted with the manual method, and the lowest for the drying/kit method (0.367).</p><p>The preservation method had a significant effect on the microsatellite PCR success rate (v 4 2 = 44.31, P = 0.00). Time-dependent decay in PCR success rate was observed for samples stored under all three preservation methods (ethanol, v 4 2 = 11.652, P = 0.02; drying, v 4 2 = 12.696, P = 0.013; freezing, v 4 2 = 10.211, P = 0.037). For ethanol preservation, reduction was observed at 9 months relative to 1 week (P = 0.008) and 2 months (P = 0.025). The PCR success rate for samples preserved by drying was reduced significantly at 6 (P = 0.025) and 9 months (P = 0.014). Freezing also displayed a time-dependent reduction in PCR success rate over time, with significant reductions observed at 6 (P = 0.014) and 9 months (P = 0.020), and between 2 and 9 months (P = 0.034). Regardless of preservation time, the type of preservation affected the rate of ADO (v 5 2 = 12.338, P = 0.03), but the rate of FA varied nonsignificantly (v 5 2 = 10.725, P = 0.057). Feces preserved with ethanol yielded the lowest ADO and FA, followed by drying and freezing for the four preservation times (Fig. <ref type="figure">2</ref>). Feces preserved with ethanol yielded no significant increase of ADO from 1 week to 6 months preservation time (P = 0.065). Our results suggest that our optimized method may be suitable for large ungulate mammals such as equids and rhinocerotids produce large, high-fiber fecal pellets.  </p></div><figure xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0" xml:id="fig_0"><head>KitFig. 1</head><label>1</label><figDesc>Fig. 1 Comparison of amplification success rates following different storage and extraction methods</figDesc><graphic coords="2,54.11,209.76,232.08,213.96" type="bitmap" /></figure>
<figure xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0" type="table" xml:id="tab_0"><head>Table 1</head><label>1</label><figDesc>Experiment design of evaluation of fecal DNA preservation and extraction methods in Przewalski's horse</figDesc><table><row><cell>Fecal sampling</cell><cell>Preservation method</cell><cell>Preservation time</cell><cell>Extraction method</cell><cell>PCR success</cell><cell>ADO and FA</cell></row><row><cell>20 samples</cell><cell>Ethanol (E)</cell><cell>1, 2, 3, 6, 9 a</cell><cell>Manual (M) [1, 2, 3, 6, 9]</cell><cell>EM [1, 2, 3, 6, 9]</cell><cell>EM [1, 2, 3, 6]</cell></row></table></figure>
			<note xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0" place="foot" xml:id="foot_0"><p>Conservation Genet Resour (2014) 6:511-513   </p></note>
		</body>
